# Epilogue — The Square We Leave Open

Return to the data center with which we began. It is still an imagined place. This time, let the electrical connection be complete. The equipment is running, and a request has arrived.

Somewhere inside the building, a computation begins. No single person needs to follow every operation. The system works because a great deal has already been arranged: materials shaped to precise requirements, power made available, cooling maintained, programs written, shifts staffed, and failures anticipated well enough that most ordinary operations can pass unnoticed.

A result appears on a screen.

At the beginning of the book, it was possible to look at this moment and see a machine answering. It is now harder to keep the rest of the world outside the picture. The answer has passed through a history of mines, laboratories, workshops, languages, institutions, and people learning from one another. Those histories do not appear in the displayed text. They remain conditions of its appearance.

Yet tracing the dependencies outward is only half the movement. We must follow the result back into a life.

Suppose, within the same imagined scene, that someone uses it to help diagnose a problem in a pump. The suggestion may be useful. It may also be wrong. A person compares it with the equipment, checks the available evidence, and decides what to do. A repair still requires access, parts, skill, and the opportunity to stop the machine long enough to work safely.

If the repair succeeds, the achievement belongs to a connection. The model contributed something; so did the person who recognized its limits, the technician who knew the equipment, the supplier who made the part, and the earlier work preserved in the machine's design. We can identify the contribution without making one participant the author of the whole event.

The pump continues working. That is an unremarkable outcome, which is part of its importance. Much of the work that sustains a world ends in something continuing to be available. Water reaches a place it is needed. A room remains habitable. A person has time to learn because someone else has made a meal or taken over a task.

These accomplishments rarely resemble an invention announced to an audience. They nevertheless reproduce the conditions under which invention, work, and ordinary happiness are possible. A society that celebrates new capacity while neglecting repair can become impressive in what it demonstrates and fragile in what it can depend on.

Now imagine the technician leaving at the end of the day. The productive network does not end at the gate. The journey home, the condition of the home, the people awaiting care, and the possibility of rest all belong to the next day's capacity to work. They also matter for reasons that do not need to be justified by tomorrow's output. A person is more than an input awaiting renewal.

This is where the argument's measurements reach their limit. We can count energy, hours, objects, and payments. We can examine which processes continue and which fail. None of those quantities alone tells us what a life should be for. The material account makes consequences visible; people still have to judge them.

The judgment can begin with concrete questions. Does a new capacity make necessary work less exhausting? Does it widen access to something useful? Does it give people more room to refuse harmful conditions? Does it preserve the environments on which others depend? Who can challenge the account of success offered by those in charge?

These questions have no single answer for every machine or institution. An improvement can help one group and burden another. A decision can make sense under immediate pressure and create a damaging dependency. Good intentions can fail through poor understanding. That is why accountability and correction belong alongside invention in the making of a humane world.

We do not have to wait for a final theory of the universe before taking these responsibilities seriously. The unresolved origin of a physical constant does not prevent us from recognizing a broken water system. Uncertainty about consciousness does not prevent us from investigating whether a tool works reliably. The absence of a guaranteed destination does not make all destinations equally desirable.

Nor do we need to imagine ourselves outside history in order to act within it. The people who repair, teach, organize, and refuse are made by conditions they did not choose. Their actions can nevertheless change some of the conditions faced by those who follow. Dependence is the setting of their agency, not its cancellation.

The difficulty is collective. A person can see a problem clearly and lack the resources to address it. An institution can possess resources and fail to hear the person who understands the problem. A useful future requires connections between knowledge and the authority to act, between the burdens of change and the ability to bear them, between productive gains and the lives those gains are meant to improve.

Those connections can be built. They can also be broken. Their maintenance deserves the same seriousness we give to a piece of essential equipment, with an additional demand: the people living within them must be able to question their purpose and terms.

The data center's next request may help design a better component. It may help sell something unnecessary. It may support a public service, strengthen a private advantage, clarify a difficult question, or repeat a convincing error. Its technical capacity does not supply one inevitable social trajectory. The organization of its use remains unfinished work.

The same is true of the larger productive inheritance. We do not begin with an empty world. We begin with useful tools, damaged environments, accumulated knowledge, entrenched claims, and institutions that both sustain and constrain us. There are things to preserve, things to repair, and things whose continuation should be contested. Making those distinctions is more demanding than either worshiping the inheritance or imagining that everything can be rebuilt from nothing.

At the start of this project, a square with one possible value offered a small pleasure: the existing arrangement had made the next step clear. The world has offered no completed grid against which all our choices can be checked. Sometimes the inherited constraints reveal an opportunity. Sometimes they reveal a branch that should not be continued. Sometimes the next useful act is to change the arrangement that made the choice appear unavoidable.

We leave a square open, then, without treating openness as emptiness. It contains the consequences of what has already happened and the room, unevenly distributed, to do something next. Our responsibility is not to know the whole future. It is to attend to the conditions we are helping make for it.

The machine continues. A part has been repaired. Someone goes home. Somewhere else, a person learns how to do work that another person will one day depend on.

The world that made us did not finish its work, and our work does not finish the world.
